WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.032
Now we connect the concepts to database storage.

00:00:03.032 --> 00:00:07.977
Analytical meaning and storage type are related, but they are not identical.

00:00:07.977 --> 00:00:13.012
A nominal identifier can use text, UUID, or an integer key.

00:00:13.012 --> 00:00:15.722
A count often uses an integer type.

00:00:15.722 --> 00:00:19.307
Currency often needs exact numeric arithmetic.

00:00:19.307 --> 00:00:26.612
The design goal is to choose types and constraints that preserve meaning and prevent invalid data where

00:00:26.612 --> 00:00:29.827
possible. Module 2 develops this much more deeply.

00:00:29.827 --> 00:00:36.957
Connect this idea to a university analyzing student records, surveys, and learning-platform activity.

00:00:36.957 --> 00:00:42.487
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:42.487 --> 00:00:50.172
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:50.172 --> 00:00:56.092
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:56.092 --> 00:01:01.991
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
